🔐 What Are Synchronized Collections?
Synchronized collections are Java collections that are safe to use in multi-threaded environments because only one thread can access the collection at a time.

They prevent concurrent modification issues, such as:

Data corruption

ConcurrentModificationException

🧰 How to Get a Synchronized Collection?
Java provides utility methods in the Collections class:

java
Copy
Edit
List<String> syncList = Collections.synchronizedList(new ArrayList<>());
Set<String> syncSet = Collections.synchronizedSet(new HashSet<>());
Map<String, String> syncMap = Collections.synchronizedMap(new HashMap<>());
🧪 Example:
java
Copy
Edit
List<String> list = Collections.synchronizedList(new ArrayList<>());

synchronized (list) {  // Manual synchronization during iteration
    for (String item : list) {
        System.out.println(item);
    }
}
🔸 Always use synchronized block when iterating, to avoid concurrency issues.

🧩 Common Synchronized Collections in Java
| Type        | Synchronized Version                |
| ----------- | ----------------------------------- |
| `List`      | `Collections.synchronizedList(...)` |
| `Set`       | `Collections.synchronizedSet(...)`  |
| `Map`       | `Collections.synchronizedMap(...)`  |
| `Vector`    | Already synchronized                |
| `Hashtable` | Already synchronized                |


⚠️ Limitation
Not scalable for high-concurrency applications.

Use Concurrent collections instead for better performance:

ConcurrentHashMap

CopyOnWriteArrayList

🧠 Summary
| Feature       | Synchronized Collections                    |
| ------------- | ------------------------------------------- |
| Thread safety | ✅ Yes                                       |
| Performance   | ⏳ Slower (locks entire collection)          |
| Iteration     | Requires **manual sync block**              |
| Alternatives  | `ConcurrentHashMap`, `CopyOnWriteArrayList` |

